5. MACHINE LEARNING PIPELINE

Build a real and reproducible supervised learning pipeline.

5.1 Data preparation

Use Pandas and NumPy for preprocessing.

Implement:

* Numerical missing-value imputation.
* Categorical missing-value handling.
* One-hot encoding for categorical variables.
* Numerical feature preprocessing when appropriate.
* Consistent feature-column handling between training and prediction.
* Prevention of target leakage.

Use Scikit-learn Pipeline and ColumnTransformer where appropriate.

Fit all preprocessing transformations on training data only.

5.2 Feature engineering

Where the source data supports them, derive:

* Competitor price difference.
* Competitor price ratio.
* Demand-to-stock ratio.
* Historical price change.
* Sales trend.
* Seasonal indicators.
* Promotion indicators.
* Time-based features.

Handle zero stock and zero denominators safely.

Never introduce features that depend on information unavailable at prediction time.

Document the formulas and assumptions for engineered features.

5.3 Training and testing

Use a reproducible train/test split.

For ordinary independent records, use an 80% training and 20% testing split.

For time-dependent pricing data, prefer a chronological split when appropriate to avoid training on future observations and testing on the past.

Make the selected splitting method visible in the model evaluation page.

If multiple observations belong to the same product, inspect whether a grouped split or a time-based evaluation is needed.

5.4 Required models

Model A: Linear Regression

Train a Scikit-learn LinearRegression model.

Display the model’s evaluation metrics.

Model B: Decision Tree Regressor

Train a DecisionTreeRegressor.

Allow sensible configuration of parameters such as:

* Maximum depth.
* Minimum samples per leaf.
* Random seed.

Display its evaluation metrics.

Model C: Random Forest Regressor

Train a RandomForestRegressor.

Allow sensible configuration of:

* Number of trees.
* Maximum depth.
* Minimum samples per leaf.
* Random seed.

Display its evaluation metrics.

Use reasonable default settings that work on ordinary student-project hardware.

Avoid unnecessary parallel processing that could overwhelm the user’s machine.

5.5 Model comparison

Build a comparison table containing:

Model	MAE	RMSE	R²	Training Status
Linear Regression	Calculated	Calculated	Calculated	Actual status
Decision Tree	Calculated	Calculated	Calculated	Actual status
Random Forest	Calculated	Calculated	Calculated	Actual status

Never hardcode these values.

Lower MAE and RMSE generally indicate lower prediction error.

Higher R² generally indicates a better fit, although R² can be negative on test data.

Show the model ranking and explain the ranking criteria.

Use MAE as the primary ranking metric, with RMSE and R² as supporting metrics, unless the user selects another documented ranking method.

Explain the limitations of model comparisons on small, noisy, or synthetic datasets.

5.6 Model persistence

Allow trained models to be saved and reloaded locally.

Save the preprocessing pipeline with the estimator so predictions use exactly the transformations applied during training.

Record:

* Model type.
* Training date.
* Dataset identifier.
* Feature schema.
* Evaluation metrics.
* Training configuration.

Validate compatibility before loading a saved model.

Never silently use a stale model trained on a different feature schema.

Treat model files as trusted local artifacts and do not load arbitrary uploaded serialized model files.

